The Balance Sheet
==================
Live site: https://thebalancesheet.netlify.app
Deploy: push to GitHub main -> Netlify builds and deploys automatically.

FILES
-----
index.html            Landing page ("Enter Site")
home.html             Main homepage
explore.html          Archive/category page + full post list
meet-the-team.html    About page
contact.html          Contact page + form
post.html             Renders one full post (reads ?post=... from the URL)
style.css             All styling (one shared stylesheet)
script.js             Scroll animations, contact form, and post loading
posts-template.csv    Starter template to import into Google Sheets
images/               Site images

WRITING & EDITING POSTS (no code, ever, after setup)
-----------------------------------------------------
Posts live in a Google Sheet, not in the code. You edit the sheet like
a normal spreadsheet; the site reads from it automatically. Nobody
visiting the site can edit that sheet -- only whoever is signed into
the Google account that owns it (you) can change it. Visitors can only
ever read it, the same as any public blog.

ONE-TIME SETUP (about 5 minutes):

1. Go to sheets.google.com -> Blank spreadsheet.
2. File -> Import -> Upload -> pick posts-template.csv from this
   folder -> Import location: "Replace current sheet". This gives you
   the right columns plus three example posts to see the format.
3. Click Share (top right) -> under "General access" change it from
   "Restricted" to "Anyone with the link" -> make sure the role is
   "Viewer" (not Editor) -> Done.
   This is what makes it read-only to everyone but you: the world can
   only see it, you're still the only one who can type into it.
4. Copy the Sheet's ID from its URL. A Sheets URL looks like:
     https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/THIS_LONG_ID_HERE/edit
   Copy the THIS_LONG_ID_HERE part.
5. Open script.js, find this line near the top:
     const POSTS_SHEET_ID = "";
   Paste your ID between the quotes, save, then commit + push like
   normal. That's the only code edit you'll ever need to make -- it's
   a one-time connection, not part of your regular workflow.

YOUR ONGOING WORKFLOW (this is the part you'll actually use):

Open the Google Sheet whenever you want to add or edit a post. Columns:

  Title        The post title.
  Category     Must be exactly one of: Mindset & Money, Daily Finance,
               Growth & Learning (matches the colors on the Explore page).
  Date         e.g. 2026-09-29. Controls sort order (newest first).
  Summary      One or two sentences shown on the preview card.
  Image URL    Optional. Paste a direct image link, or leave blank for
               a plain color block instead.
  Published    Type "Yes" to make it go live, "No" to keep it a draft
               that only you can see in the sheet.
  Body         The full post. Press Alt+Enter (Windows) or
               Option+Enter (Mac) inside the cell to start a new
               paragraph -- each one becomes its own paragraph on the
               site.

Add a new post by adding a new row. Edit an existing post by editing
its row. Take a post down by switching Published to "No" or deleting
the row. Changes usually show up within a minute or two of reloading
the site -- no rebuild or redeploy needed, since the site fetches the
sheet live each time someone visits.

The three placeholder cards on the homepage disappear automatically
once you have at least one row with Published set to "Yes".

CONTACT FORM (no backend code needed)

CONTACT FORM (no backend code needed)
--------------------------------------
The contact form uses Netlify Forms, a feature built into Netlify
itself, so there's no server, database, or API key to manage:

1. Netlify scans contact.html when it deploys and notices the
   <form data-netlify="true"> tag.
2. Whenever someone submits the form, Netlify saves it for you.
3. View submissions any time at: Netlify dashboard -> your site ->
   "Forms".
4. To get an email every time someone submits: Netlify dashboard ->
   Forms -> Settings -> "Add notification" -> "Email notification".

Spam protection: there's a hidden "bot-field" input (the honeypot).
Real visitors never see or fill it in, but spam bots usually fill in
every field they find -- so if it's ever non-empty, Netlify quietly
throws the submission away. If spam ever becomes a real problem, you
can turn on Netlify's optional reCAPTCHA from the same Forms settings
page -- no code changes required.

Note: the form only works once it's actually deployed on Netlify --
opening contact.html directly from your computer (or a plain local
preview) won't submit anywhere, that's expected.
